@node Safety and Sandbox
@chapter Safety and Sandbox
@cindex safety
@cindex sandbox

Codebud applies two independent layers of protection before a shell command
runs.  This chapter explains what each layer does, how they interact, and
how to tune the policy for your workflow.

@menu
* Two-Layer Model::             Why there are two guards, not one.
* The Safety Engine::           JSON policy + per-step validation.
* The Sandbox::                 Subprocess guard + timeout.
* How a Command Gets Through::  The full decision path.
* Integrity Checks::            Refusing a swapped model or tool.
* Editing the Policy Files::    Adding and removing allowed commands.
* What Is Not Protected::       Honest limitations.
@end menu


@node Two-Layer Model
@section Two-Layer Model
@cindex two-layer safety

@example
LLM output
    │
    ▼
SafetyEngine.validate_step()          @r{(layer 1: policy file check)}
    │  reject → plan_refused: which step, and why
    ▼
AgentCore._validate_plan()            @r{(same check, defence-in-depth)}
    │  reject → no plan
    ▼
SafetyEngine.may_execute()            @r{(re-check right before each step)}
    │  reject → step_refused
    ▼
Executor → tool.run(args)
    │
    ▼  (for command steps only)
catastrophic_reason()                 @r{(layer 2: parsed backstop, again)}
    │  block → returncode=1
    ▼
subprocess.run(shlex.split(cmd), timeout=10)
@end example

The two layers are independent.  Layer 1 is policy-driven and editable.
Layer 2 is hard-coded and cannot be overridden from config files.  Both
must pass for a shell command to run.

Non-shell tools (@code{file_write}, @code{file_read}, @code{patch},
@code{web_search}, @code{search_embeddings}) pass directly
through layer 1 and bypass layer 2 entirely.


@node The Safety Engine
@section The Safety Engine
@cindex SafetyEngine
@cindex validate_step

@code{SafetyEngine.validate_step(step)} is called by @code{LLMPlanner}
during plan validation, again by @code{AgentCore}, and once more by
@code{Executor} (through @code{may_execute()}) right before each step runs,
so a plan made in one agent mode cannot run after switching to a stricter
one.
For @code{command} steps it applies one rule:

@quotation
The @code{cmd} value, split into words the same way the sandbox splits
it, must begin with all the words of an entry in
@file{harmless_commands.json} or @file{powerful_commands.json}.
@end quotation

Matching is by whole words, not string prefixes: @code{"ls -la"} matches
the entry @code{"ls"}, and @code{"git log -n 3"} matches @code{"git log"};
but @code{"lsblk"} does not match @code{"ls"}, @code{"catastrophe"} does not
match @code{"cat"}, and @code{"git push"} does not match @code{"git log"}.
A path-qualified program (@code{/bin/ls}) or a command that cannot be
parsed (unbalanced quotes) is rejected.

@cindex refused arguments
Some arguments are refused even when the program is listed, because they
turn it into ``run anything'' or ``delete or overwrite something'':

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .78
@headitem Program @tab Refused arguments
@item @command{find} @tab @option{-exec}, @option{-execdir}, @option{-ok},
@option{-okdir} (run programs), @option{-delete}, @option{-fprint},
@option{-fprint0}, @option{-fprintf}, @option{-fls} (delete or write files)
@item @command{python}, @command{python3} @tab @option{-c} (inline code),
including combined forms such as @option{-Ic}; only the interpreter's own
options are checked, so @code{python3 script.py -c config.toml} is allowed
@item @command{git} @tab @option{--output} (writes a file),
@option{--ext-diff} (runs an external diff program)
@item @command{awk} @tab @option{-f} (program from a file), and a program
that runs a command: @code{system()}, a @code{getline} from a command, or
a @code{print} piped to one
@item @command{sed} @tab @option{-f} (script from a file), the @code{e}
command (runs a shell command), and @code{w}, @code{W} or the @code{s///w}
flag (write a file); the script is parsed so an ordinary @code{w} inside a
substitution is not refused
@item @command{nc}, @command{ncat}, @command{netcat} @tab @option{-e},
@option{-c}, @option{--exec}, @option{--sh-exec}, @option{--lua-exec}
(run a program on the connection)
@item @command{tar} @tab @option{--to-command}, @option{-I},
@option{--use-compress-program}, @option{--checkpoint-action},
@option{-F}, @option{--info-script}, @option{--new-volume-script},
@option{--rmt-command}, @option{--rsh-command} (run programs),
@option{--remove-files} (deletes what it archives)
@item @command{rsync} @tab @option{-e}, @option{--rsh} (run a program)
@item @command{sort} @tab @option{--compress-program} (runs a program)
@item @command{journalctl} @tab @option{--rotate}, @option{--vacuum-*},
@option{--flush}, @option{--relinquish-var}, @option{--sync} (change or
delete logs)
@item @command{env} @tab @option{-S}, @option{--split-string} (turns one
argument into a whole command line, out of sight)
@end multitable

These are matched the way the programs parse their options:
@option{--opt} and @option{--opt=@var{value}}, and any abbreviation of
a long option, which GNU programs accept (@option{--to-com} is
@option{--to-command}); a short option alone, with
its value attached (@option{-e/bin/sh}) or among others
(@option{-ve}); nothing after @option{--}; and @command{tar}'s old style,
where a first argument without a dash is a group of options
(@code{tar cIf} is @code{tar -c -I -f}).  A short option found inside a
group is refused even where it is in fact another option's value: the
check errs on the side of refusing.  A program given by its full path
(@file{/usr/bin/nc}) is judged by its name, and a versioned or
distribution-specific name by its family (@command{python3.14} as
@command{python}, @command{nc.openbsd} and @command{nc.traditional} as
@command{nc}).  Every word of the command line that names a program with
such rules is judged where it stands, so whatever runs its arguments as
a command is seen through without being listed: @code{timeout 5 nc -e
@dots{}}, @code{env X=1 python3 -c @dots{}}, @code{xargs -a list python3
-c @dots{}}, @code{taskset -c 0 @dots{}}, @code{strace -f @dots{}}.  The
cost: a program named only as data and followed by one of its refused
options is refused too --- @code{grep -e python -c log} reads as
@code{python -c}; @code{grep -c -e python log} does the same and is
allowed.  A program with such rules run by @command{xargs} or
@command{parallel} is refused outright: they add arguments read from a
file or from input, which cannot be checked (@code{xargs -a files.txt
wc -l} is fine).

@cindex imaging tools, rules
@cindex dc3dd
@cindex ewfacquire
@cindex exiftool
Three tools get rules a list of options cannot express.  They apply
wherever a profile allow-lists the tool (@code{security-ir} does) and
keep it to working on evidence as NIST SP 800-86 (section 4.2.2) and the
ACPO principles ask: on copies, hashed, logged, never writing to a
device or outside the project.

@table @command
@item dc3dd
Its input (@option{if=}, @option{ifs=}) must be an image file inside the
project, never a device; every output and log (@option{of=},
@option{hof=}, @option{ofs=}, @option{hofs=}, @option{log=},
@option{hlog=}, @option{mlog=}) inside the project; @option{hash=} and
@option{log=} or @option{hlog=} required.  @option{wipe=},
@option{hwipe=}, @option{pat=} and @option{tpat=} are refused, and so is
any key not known to be safe.
@item ewfacquire
Unattended (@option{-u}), since nobody can answer its prompts in a step,
and so with the case metadata on the command line: @option{-C} (case),
@option{-e} (examiner), @option{-E} (evidence number), @option{-D}
(description); @option{-d} including @code{sha256}; a log
(@option{-l}); the target (@option{-t}) and any second target
(@option{-2}) inside the project; the source one image file inside the
project.  Options not known to be safe are refused.
@item exiftool
Only reading.  Refused: an argument with @samp{=}, @samp{<} or @samp{>}
(writing, copying or renaming tags), one with @samp{$@{} (Perl code),
and the options that write, run code or read arguments from a file
(@option{-o}, @option{-overwrite_original}, @option{-tagsFromFile},
@option{-geotag}, @option{-p}, @option{-if}, @option{-config},
@option{-api}, @option{-@@}, @option{-w}, @option{-execute}, @dots{}).
Read options and tag names (@code{exiftool -a -G1 -s},
@code{exiftool -Artist photo.jpg}) are fine.
@end table

@noindent
Every output, target and log must be a new file: one that already
exists --- the input image, another piece of evidence, an earlier
@file{.E01} --- is refused, so an imaging step can never overwrite
evidence.  The paths are judged with symbolic links followed, so a link
inside the project that points out of it does not count as inside.  These rules
were written from the tools' manual pages.

@cindex refused_arguments.json
You can refuse more with @file{refused_arguments.json}, which maps a
program to the options it may never be given:

@example
@{
  "curl": ["-T", "--upload-file"],
  "nmap": ["-iR", "--script-args-file"],
  "journalctl": ["--vacuum-*"]
@}
@end example

@noindent
Every such file in force counts: the active profile's policy directory,
your @file{~/.config/codebud/} and the project's @file{.codebud/} are
combined, and each can only add refusals --- none can remove one,
including those in the table above.  A project needs no trust for it,
since adding refusals only narrows.  An entry that is not a program name
and a list of options (@samp{-x}, @samp{--long}, @samp{--prefix-*}) is
ignored with a warning, and @command{codebud doctor} fails on a file
that is not valid JSON.  What it cannot cover: a program that reads its
options from a configuration file or the environment.

When a step fails validation the entire plan is rejected and the agent
never executes a partial plan.  A rejection caused only by the agent mode
is reported as such (@samp{The plan uses tools not allowed in edit mode:
command.}), and so is a policy rejection, naming the step and the reason
(@samp{The safety policy refused step 1 (du -sh src): du is not on the
allow-list}; @pxref{Safety Rejection}).  The same reasons are given when a
step is refused right before it runs.

@code{describe_rules()} returns a compact summary of the three policy
lists, which is embedded in the planner prompt so the model can avoid
proposing forbidden commands in the first place.

@noindent
Source: @file{src/codebud/safety.py}


@node The Sandbox
@section The Sandbox
@cindex catastrophic commands
@cindex hard-blocked commands

The allow-list is the main gate, but you can widen it (your own policy, a
trusted project, a profile).  So a small set of catastrophic, irreversible
commands is refused whatever the allow-list says --- by the safety policy
when the plan is checked, and by the @code{Sandbox} again right before
running:

@table @asis
@item Powering off
@command{shutdown}, @command{reboot}, @command{poweroff}, @command{halt},
and @command{systemctl} @code{poweroff}/@code{reboot}/@code{halt}/@code{kexec}.
@item Erasing a disk
@command{mkfs} (any variant), @command{wipefs}, and @command{dd} writing
to a device (@code{of=/dev/@dots{}}).
@item Deleting a vital directory
@command{rm} with any recursive option (@option{-r}, @option{-R},
@option{-rf}, @option{-fr}, @option{--recursive}) on @file{/}, a top-level
system directory (@file{/usr}, @file{/etc}, @file{/home}, @dots{}), or a
user's home directory (yours, @file{/home/@var{name}}, @file{/root},
@file{/Users/@var{name}}); and @option{--no-preserve-root} anywhere.
@end table

@noindent
The command is judged on its parsed words, so spacing and option order do
not matter (@code{rm -fr /}, @code{rm  -rf /}), and wrapper commands are
looked through (@command{sudo}, @command{env @var{X}=@var{y}},
@command{timeout 5}, @command{nice}, @dots{}).  Steps run without a shell,
so nothing is expanded: @code{rm -rf ~} or @code{rm -rf *} names a file
literally called @file{~} or @file{*}, and there is no shell to run a fork
bomb in.  A refused step returns @code{returncode=1} and a @code{stderr}
message without touching anything.  This is a backstop for the worst
cases, not a sandbox: what isolation and the allow-list do not prevent,
it does not either (@pxref{What Is Not Protected}).

@strong{Timeout.}  Every command runs under a 10-second wall-clock timeout
enforced by @code{subprocess.run(timeout=10)}.  A timed-out command
returns @code{returncode=1} with the message
@samp{Command timed out after 10 seconds}.

@strong{No shell expansion.}  The command string is split with
@code{shlex.split()} and passed as a list to @code{subprocess.run()}.
Shell metacharacters (@code{&&}, @code{|}, @code{;}, @code{>}) are treated
as literal argument characters, not operators, so @code{ls; rm -rf ~} is
just @command{ls} with two odd arguments.  Pipelines are not supported:
@code{sh} is not on the allow-list.

@strong{Isolation.}  At privacy level @code{private} (the default) and
@code{locked}, every command runs isolated with bubblewrap: no network
unless the step declared it, the file system read-only except the project
and a fresh @file{/tmp}, and your secrets hidden (@pxref{Isolated Steps}).

@noindent
Source: @file{src/codebud/sandbox.py}, @file{src/codebud/isolation.py}


@node How a Command Gets Through
@section How a Command Gets Through

The following table traces the path of a @code{command} step from LLM
output to execution.

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .20 .55
@headitem Command @tab Result @tab Reason
@item @code{ls -la} @tab @kbd{executes} @tab first word is @code{ls}, a harmless entry
@item @code{lsblk} @tab @kbd{rejected} @tab @code{lsblk} is not @code{ls}: whole words must match
@item @code{git log -n 3} @tab @kbd{executes} @tab begins with the words @code{git log}
@item @code{git push} @tab @kbd{rejected} @tab only @code{git log}, @code{git status}, @code{git diff}, @code{git show} are listed
@item @code{cp foo bar} @tab @kbd{executes} @tab @code{cp} is a powerful entry
@item @code{rm file.txt} @tab @kbd{rejected} @tab @code{rm} is in neither list
@item @code{rm -rf /} @tab @kbd{blocked} @tab catastrophic: refused regardless of policy
@item @code{sudo pacman -Syu} @tab @kbd{rejected} @tab @code{sudo} is in neither list
@end multitable

@noindent
While an integrity manifest exists, a step that passes these checks is
checked once more right before it runs (@pxref{Integrity Checks}).


@node Integrity Checks
@section Integrity Checks
@cindex integrity checks
@cindex integrity.json
@cindex manifest, integrity

The allow-list says which programs a step may run; it cannot tell
whether @command{git} is still the @command{git} you installed, or whether
the model behind a name was replaced.  @command{codebud integrity pin}
records both (@pxref{The integrity Subcommand}): the Ollama digest of
the chat and embedding models, and the real path and SHA-256 of every
program on the allow-list and of the isolation tool.

While that manifest, @file{~/.config/codebud/integrity.json}, exists,
@command{run}, @command{plan} and @command{chat} check it before they do
anything else, and refuse to start on any difference:

@itemize
@item the model to be used (@option{--model}, else @env{OLLAMA_MODEL})
is not pinned, its digest changed, or Ollama cannot be asked;
@item project search is on and the embedding model is not pinned or
changed (@option{--no-rag} leaves it out);
@item a pinned program's file changed, is gone, or the name now
resolves to another file on @env{PATH};
@item a file of the security layer's profiles changed or is gone, so an
edited profile or policy cannot loosen a security profile unnoticed.
@end itemize

@noindent
A model taken into use later is checked the same way: switching models
in @command{chat} (@code{/model}), turning project search on with
@code{/rag on}, and building or refreshing the index
(@command{codebud index}, @code{/index}), which embeds with the
embedding model, are refused on a mismatch.  Each @code{command} step is checked again right before it runs:
the program it executes, the one a wrapper such as @command{timeout}
runs, and the isolation tool must all be pinned and unchanged, or the
step is refused and nothing runs --- including a program that is on the
allow-list but was added after the last pin.  The step then runs each of
them by the full path that was checked, so a name cannot find another
file inside the isolation sandbox (where @file{/tmp} is empty and some
directories are hidden).  A program given as a
relative path that resolves inside the project (symbolic links
followed), such as @command{./configure}, is a file of the project,
which the manifest does not cover; the allow-list alone decides those.
A relative path that leads out of the project (@file{../tool}) is
checked like any other program.  A manifest that cannot be read is an error, never a reason to skip
the check, and a profile with @code{integrity = true} refuses to start,
or to be switched to with @code{/profile}, while nothing is pinned
(@pxref{Profiles}).

When the change is yours --- @command{ollama pull}, a system update ---
check it with @command{codebud integrity verify}, then pin again.

What this does not cover: the manifest lives in your account, so anyone
who can change your files can also re-pin it; a program checked before
it runs can still read scripts or libraries that are not pinned
(@command{python3} is pinned, the script it runs is not); and a file
rewritten in the instant between its check and its start is not caught.

@node Editing the Policy Files
@section Editing the Policy Files
@cindex editing policy files

Copy the default @file{harmless_commands.json} or
@file{powerful_commands.json} to @file{~/.config/codebud/} (for all
projects) or to a project's @file{.codebud/} (for that project only; it
widens your policy only once you trust the project, @pxref{Project
Policy}) and edit the copy with any text editor (@pxref{Policy JSON
Files}).  Changes
take effect the next time @code{AgentCore} is initialised (i.e.@: the next
@command{codebud} invocation); @command{codebud config} shows which file is
in effect.

@subsection Recommended configuration for Python development

The defaults already cover a Python workflow: read-only tools and
@code{git log}/@code{status}/@code{diff}/@code{show} are harmless (and run
with the repository's own hooks, filters and diff/textconv drivers
disabled, so opening a repository someone else prepared cannot run their
programs), and the code runners (@code{python}, @code{python3}, @code{pytest}, @code{ruff}) are
powerful, so @code{auto} mode confirms them.  Typical additions:

@example
@r{~/.config/codebud/powerful_commands.json:}
[
  "grep", "sed", "awk", "cp", "mv",
  "python", "python3", "pytest", "ruff",
  "mkdir", "touch", "pip install", "git add", "git commit"
]
@end example

@subsection Tips

@itemize
@item
Add @code{"git log"} rather than @code{"git"} to avoid inadvertently
permitting @command{git push} or @command{git reset --hard}.

@item
Put anything that runs code or changes files in the powerful list, not
the harmless one; only powerful commands (and web searches, which send
data off the machine) make @code{auto} mode and @command{codebud run}
(without @option{--yes}) stop and ask.

@item
After editing, run @command{codebud doctor} to confirm the files parse
correctly.

@item
Entries match whole words.  @code{"python"} allows @code{python script.py}
but not @code{python3}; list each program you want to allow.  A multi-word
entry such as @code{"git log"} allows only that subcommand.
@end itemize


@node What Is Not Protected
@section What Is Not Protected
@cindex limitations, safety

The safety system is designed to prevent accidental mistakes by a
well-intentioned model, not to defend against an adversarial one.  Be
aware of the following limitations:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Prompt injection is made harder, not impossible.}  A file in your
project can contain text aimed at the model, and through the project
index it reaches the planning prompt.  (Step output and web search
results are shown to you and stored, but never sent back to the model.)
Codebud fences project context with a random tag
per request (a file cannot fake its end or pose as your request), labels
lines that imitate the prompt's structure as @samp{(file text)}, and
keeps the model's own step descriptions out of the history, recording
what each step does instead.  A model can still be persuaded by text it
reads.  What protects you then is the rest of this chapter: the plan
shows the real file, command and query of every step before it runs,
the allow-list and the catastrophic-command refusal apply, file tools
are confined to the project, and command steps are isolated.

@item
@strong{File tools are confined, not isolated.}  Isolation applies to
@code{command} steps, which run as separate processes.
@code{file_read}, @code{file_write} and @code{patch} run inside Codebud
itself, so their paths are checked instead (symlinks resolved first):
@code{file_write} and @code{patch} may only write inside the project, and
never into its @file{.git/} (hooks and config run programs) or
@file{.codebud/} (the project's command policy); @code{file_read} may
read outside the project, as @command{cat} may, but never the secret
files isolation hides (@file{~/.ssh}, @file{~/.gnupg}, @file{.netrc},
@dots{}) or Codebud's own data, configuration and keys.  A step that
breaks these rules is refused before it runs.  Within those limits they
use your rights: a write inside the project is a real write.

@item
@strong{A network step shares more.}  A step that declares the network
shares the host's network namespace, so besides the internet it can
reach services listening on this machine (@code{localhost}) and
abstract Unix sockets, such as an X11 display.  Steps that do not
declare the network have neither.

@item
@strong{Running Codebud on its own checkout.}  You may use Codebud on its
own source --- an editable install with its @file{.venv} inside the
repository, say.  There, an approved edit or step can change the code the
next Codebud run executes, its safety checks included (@file{safety.py},
@file{policy.py}, @file{isolation.py}, or a package installed into the
environment).  Codebud allows it and says so at startup when its own code
or Python environment is inside the project; review such changes with
that in mind.  For untrusted repositories, use a separate installation
(@command{pipx install}, or one outside the project).

@item
@strong{Network only where isolation works.}  With bubblewrap, command
steps have no network unless they declare it; at privacy level
@code{trusted}, or where isolation is unavailable at @code{private}, they
can reach the network (@pxref{Isolated Steps}).

@item
@strong{No privilege escalation guard.}  @code{"sudo"} is not in the
default policy, but if you add it, any @code{sudo} command the model
proposes will run.

@item
@strong{Code runners can do anything.}  The refused arguments above stop
the obvious one-liners (@code{python -c}, @code{awk 'BEGIN@{system(...)@}'},
@code{sed '1e ...'}), but an entry of @code{"python"} still allows
@code{python evil.py} --- and the model can write @file{evil.py} first
with @code{file_write} inside the project.  This is why @code{python},
@code{python3}, @code{pytest}, @code{ruff}, @code{awk} and @code{sed} are
powerful commands that need confirmation (except in @code{full-auto} mode
or with @code{run --yes}).  The refused arguments reduce the unattended
blast radius; they are not a sandbox.

@item
@strong{10-second timeout is short.}  Long operations (building a large
project, running a slow test suite) will time out.  Adjust the timeout in
@file{src/codebud/sandbox.py} if needed.
@end itemize

@noindent
@strong{Bottom line:} Codebud is a developer tool, not a security boundary.
Run it in a directory you own, with files you are willing to modify, and
under a user account that does not have elevated privileges.
